{"qid":"Q105092","names":["Kingdom of Mercia"],"title":"Kingdom of Mercia","endonym":{"native":"Miercna Rīce","lang":"ang"},"capsule":"Mercia was one of the major Anglo-Saxon kingdoms of central England, emerging in the early sixth century from a confederation of Anglian tribes settled in the Midlands. It reached its apex under Offa (757–796), who dominated most of southern Britain, corresponded with Charlemagne as an equal, and commissioned the great dyke along the Welsh border that still bears his name. After Offa's death the kingdom fragmented; the Vikings of the Danelaw overran eastern Mercia in the 870s, and the rump western kingdom was absorbed into the emerging English state under Æthelflæd, Lady of the Mercians, whose death in 918 left it fully subject to Wessex, with nominal continuity as an earldom ending by 926.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. Mercia's core territory in the English Midlands is well-attested through charters, Bede, and the Anglo-Saxon Chronicle, though precise boundaries fluctuated considerably with conquest and political alliance.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Britannica: Mercia","url":"https://www.britannica.com/place/Mercia"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Mercia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mercia"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Offa of Mercia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Offa_of_Mercia"}],"britannica":"place/Mercia","cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Mercia","rulers":[{"qid":"Q738812","name":"Icel","start":515,"end":535,"image":null},{"qid":"Q2430124","name":"Cnebba","start":535,"end":545,"image":null},{"qid":"Q2642796","name":"Cynewald","start":545,"end":580,"image":null}